  "habitat": "Flocks, often large, in grassland and farmland. They perch on power lines and mix with Lesser Kestrels and Red-footed Falcons.",
  "summary": "A common summer visitor from northern and eastern Asia. It arrives in late spring and leaves by April or May. Both sexes are grey above, and the soft parts are red or orange. The male is pale grey below with a rufous vent and white underwing coverts. The female is white and well spotted, including the underwing coverts. The immature is greyer and less rufous than an immature Red-footed Falcon. Newman does not describe the voice. Months were left unset. The Kemp name is Eastern Redfooted Kestrel and the code NB was left unchanged.",
